ing because  the game's setting the kingdom of hermes  is beset by a devastating plague along  with the plague killing and mutating  people there is also a presence of  alchemical research focusing largely on  two kinds of blood pure and vile which  is used in experiments and to strengthen  the player character corvus himself is a  result of this alchemical  experimentation playing as the crow man  is fun your combat kit from dodge to  slash to parry to claw to feather makes  for a dynamic toolset which keeps fights  high paced the player generally always  has something to do and they can choose  which avenue they want to go down  playing more passive and dodging playing  more aggressive and parrying or  something in between the combat can  really soar and i'll talk more about  that when i get into the details of how  it all works  for now i'll leave it with those  positive words to talk about the issues  of the game which is almost everything  else to start there are only three main  levels instead of an open or connected  world thymesia gives instance levels in  which you have to carry out missions  when you complete a level's main mission  you unlock the next level along with a  few optional missions sometimes there  are even more optional missions that  open up after completing preliminary  ones all in all there are three main  levels along with one final level which  is essentially just the final boss fight  in the three levels there are 12  missions in total with three core  missions and nine optional ones  just playing the core missions which  include three bosses plus the final boss  i finished a quick run-through of the  game in less than 1 hour with the  initial playthrough at less than 10  hours this should show you how short of  a game thymija is  this level formula works as a way to  increase the content in the game without  drastically increasing the development  work and that makes sense for the small  development team however the game is  already short so replaying levels  definitely takes a bit out of the  experience to be fair a lot of them do  situate you differently so you progress  in different ways or explore parts of  the level that aren't present in the  rest of the other missions there are  even one or two missions that do have  their complete own zone like their own  level or mini level so the  implementation isn't terrible but it is  still a bit lackluster and kind of  monotonous  there is also no new game plus at the  moment so what you have here is the  entirety of what you get you are able to  go back and replay all the boss fights  which is a definite plus and the game  even pushes you to do so to get certain  upgrades so you get to feel your  progression as you get more familiar  with the game which it shows each time  you face the boss there's an achievement  to beat a boss without healing and i  eventually went back and got that on the  first proper boss of the game which is  one of the more difficult ones and that  felt quite satisfying to do given my  original high death count to him  along with the level reuse the enemy  variety is also not stellar you'll find  similar night enemies and the same kind  of dude with sword for most of the game  as you progress you do come across  knights with special weapon types which  make for different gameplay but it's not  quite enough to keep things new  occasionally there is a more unique foe  like the whip enemy for instance and  these are a breath of fresh air that the  game could have used a good amount more  of these issues along with others like  no voice acting bosses being a little  bit too easy and a slight disconnect  between the lore and the realization of  it in enemies all detract from the game  bamija could have achieved a  comprehensive soul's effect even if on a  smaller scale but the lack of developed  content across multiple areas of the  game prevents this i enjoy the story and  lore concepts especially in the  pureblood vile blood dic