 by the game's leveling system  which takes a more streamlined approach  compared to the dozen or so stats  available for upgrade in souls games  instead there are just three stats to  choose from strength vitality and plague  upgrading strength increases your  sword's attack power upgrading vitality  gives you more health and upgrading play  gives you more energy and makes your  claw do more damage to wounds each time  you gain a level here you get one skill  point to invest in your various skilled  trees these can be changed at any time  so you are free to experiment with them  and they range in kind from increasing  your damage or feather count to giving  you new abilities counters and buffs to  return to energy and feathers energy is  what you expend when using plague  weapons which are basically special  kinds of attacks that you can unlock and  upgrade by defeating enemies who use the  same weapons a bow enemy can drop  materials which let you unlock the bow  plague weapon a spear enemy the spear  plague weapon and so on these plague  weapons have cooldowns and energy costs  but you can get energy back through the  use of your claw and potions others are  projectiles which slowly regenerate  after use and they are first introduced  to let you counter enemy special attacks  one of their skill upgrades gives you a  dash to close distance between you and  your enemy and this was the main reason  i use them in my playthrough eventually  you can unlock perks which let you  basically makiri counter the special  attacks or just parry them and i ended  up relying on these methods instead of  the feathers  so those are the core gameplay mechanics  there are also health charges in the  form of potions and you can unlock three  different kinds you start with normal  and you can get slow ones which heal  over time or fast ones which heal  instantly and can be used quicker i'm a  fan of the fast ones as they feel like  blood vials and they can help keep up  the pace during fights you can upgrade  these potions with materials from  killing strong enemies and you can use  ingredients to give them special effects  like increased damage or resistance  now you'll understand most of what  happens on the screen this system feels  the most fleshed out of anything in the  game to me it makes sense that the  combat is the central focus and i think  overboarder studio the dev team did a  good job with their own implementation  of this kind of action rpg there can  occasionally be some issues with getting  locked into punished chains from enemies  even if you input correctly on a second  or third hit if you get hit on the first  hit but overall the kit that give the  player is pretty comprehensive and  satisfying to use once you get your  bearings learning enemy movesets and  becoming comfortable enough to dodge  into and parry enemy attacks while  keeping up your own slashes and claws  makes for a satisfying and engaging  combat flow you have to decide your  openings to attack in but parries can  come out blisteringly fast when you're  using your claw claw claw parry claw  becomes your punished dance and it feels  great when you get it working especially  against someone like vark really the  only issue here other than the  aforementioned stickiness of some enemy  punishes is that when you get into the  flow and comfortable with a boss they  often get too easy i think some more  mix-ups or even letting bosses  occasionally parry the player would help  or if bosses had some kind of claw  counter it would help keep things more  engaging and increase the skill ceiling  while there are definitely elements and  enemies in the game that are challenging  when you're learning them the switch  sometimes flips too quickly from  difficult to sailing through the enemy  with ease overall though the combat is  pretty great and it's what kept me  hooked into the game  unfortunately most of the game is not up  to par with this system and this  detracts from th