atic notes for  the player to feel difference in them  the similar dankness between levels 1  and 2 did not really achieve that for me  i will say that i like the lore of the  game it impacts which ending you get and  it is dependent on you completing each  mission and searching out an npc to get  answers it adds to the world building  and reveals a deeper story to most of  the game's major players there are  conspiracies and secret research  projects of injecting pure and vile  blood into soldiers and animals it fits  and adds to the plague elements though  the lack of different enemies to flesh  out the lore hurts for example in the  note to the last level it says that  there are two kinds of knights in the  kingdom's fortress the knights of the  pureblood and the royal guard these two  groups came up as distinct in the lore  and they serve different purposes but  none of the game's basic enemies or  knights bear any distinction so when i  fought through the fortress i never  experienced fighting one group and then  the other there was no difference in the  enemies at least that i could see and  that meant the interesting lore didn't  get to complement the gameplay like it  could have it didn't change the feeling  of killing the third bowman or the 15th  knight with a sword and shield  lastly i want to talk about the final  boss so spoiler warning if you don't  want to spoil yourself then go to this  time in the video  okay so the final boss of the game is a  mimic fight you fight yourself corvus  and he is equipped with a kit that you  have been amassing throughout the game i  don't think the boss's moveset is  actually dependent on what you unlock or  use but chances are you will unlock  almost everything if you play through  all of the game's missions the fight is  interesting because you get to put to  the test everything you've learned both  in offense and defense but it's also  lackluster because we don't get anything  new for the final boss of the game it's  just a rehash of a bunch of moves that  we are already familiar with there are  lots of combat tools to engage with such  as the parry and claw so it makes for a  better one-on-one than say eldon rings  mimic boss fight but it pales in  comparison to something like ishin and  sekiro where we get that kind of duel  between equals but still have a wide  variety of new moves and attacks that  add to the fight's engagement factor and  its spectacle maybe it would have been  better to have corvus be a phase one and  then grow to something more unique in  phase two like turn more crow than human  undergo a dark sinister transformation  that could fit with the corrupting and  empowering blood motifs that are present  in the lore and we do see something  similar to that with one of the earlier  bosses it's just that the game separates  it into two different fights the game  could in general have more amped up  phase 2s for bosses that would help with  the difficulty and this fight especially  could use the buff to make it something  a lot more distinct and memorable it  could even have been a better fight if  boss corvus could use perry so you can't  just spam attack at him during any  opening having a little bit of this  would keep the player a bit more on edge  and it would feel better and more  natural since we have a parry and we're  supposed to be fighting ourself but boss  corvus never parries us  overall thymesia shines during some of  the boss fights but the lack of  development in most other areas  especially enemy variety holds it back i  had fun with the game i enjoyed a bit of  the exploration and the fights but i  didn't get much more of a cohesive  experience other than those few moments  where the dance of combat really clicked  and one final note i forgot to mention  earlier the soundtrack to the game is  fine the main theme is fitting and  grandiose in the right way but most of  the others are somewhat forgettable to  me i will say that the os stage to th